{:edb/db-before-t 2616 :edb/db-after-t 2617 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535929 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:52:40.688Z" 13194139535929 true] [17592186129099 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375017 13194139535929 true] [17592186129141 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374998 13194139535929 true] [17592186133800 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375007 13194139535929 true] [17592186133812 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374995 13194139535929 true] [17592186133850 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375014 13194139535929 true] [17592186133854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375001 13194139535929 true] [17592186198312 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375004 13194139535929 true] [17592186374995 1017 #edb/ref 17592186374996 13194139535929 true] [17592186374995 1089 "q-168615" 13194139535929 true] [17592186374995 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535929 true] [17592186374995 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139535929 true] [17592186374995 1113 #uuid "d208a446-252e-5962-98f7-431e5b98dc76" 13194139535929 true] [17592186374995 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2x+0 \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535929 true] [17592186374996 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374997 13194139535929 true] [17592186374996 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535929 true] [17592186374996 1041 "field-1" 13194139535929 true] [17592186374996 1104 #edb/ref 17592186374997 13194139535929 true] [17592186374996 1110 #uuid "83e57f55-2229-5b7f-9155-1498080145f3" 13194139535929 true] [17592186374997 1034 "2x" 13194139535929 true] [17592186374997 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535929 true] [17592186374997 1111 #uuid "e3f7d789-7fe7-56e3-a007-c3a764e6bcf3" 13194139535929 true] [17592186374998 1017 #edb/ref 17592186374999 13194139535929 true] [17592186374998 1089 "q-168616" 13194139535929 true] [17592186374998 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535929 true] [17592186374998 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the volume of the region shown above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Volume $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139535929 true] [17592186374998 1113 #uuid "f84fc6b5-0699-5ceb-9dc7-5f701d2d5fa3" 13194139535929 true] [17592186374998 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/dc432b01077dc153940a88689f398283c09ee2a9d3385f4619a2e4a12cfa9c10.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-y},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/fcb2a304b7333a8ee3d2ae38c26b337fa4e486f0d33034b68982dc9f2cc0bb11.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-x-y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[(4-x)y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((4-x)-\\frac{1}{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{7}{2}-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{7x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535929 true] [17592186374999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375000 13194139535929 true] [17592186374999 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535929 true] [17592186374999 1041 "field-1" 13194139535929 true] [17592186374999 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375000 13194139535929 true] [17592186374999 1110 #uuid "67229e62-dd53-573b-adcf-192d051bb308" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375000 1034 "3" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375000 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535929 true] [17592186375000 1111 #uuid "7aa80643-af7b-58ec-b9e6-62aa28b5ef73" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375001 1017 #edb/ref 17592186375002 13194139535929 true] [17592186375001 1089 "q-168617" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375001 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535929 true] [17592186375001 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the volume of the region shown above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Volume $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nLet $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate is ${∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375001 1113 #uuid "768c2a7d-6a51-5200-ac90-d2b7874a059f" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375001 1145 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nIn part 1, we found that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375002 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375003 13194139535929 true] [17592186375002 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535929 true] [17592186375002 1041 "field-1" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375002 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375003 13194139535929 true] [17592186375002 1110 #uuid "90629955-3091-5dbc-8451-60186f068b6a" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375003 1034 "2x" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375003 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535929 true] [17592186375003 1111 #uuid "dd220b49-a468-509f-b367-91fe81f808b5" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375004 1017 #edb/ref 17592186375005 13194139535929 true] [17592186375004 1089 "q-168618" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375004 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535929 true] [17592186375004 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the volume of the region shown above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Volume $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nLet $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate is ${∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375004 1113 #uuid "aed15fbe-5e59-5d92-bf7c-ed947204cd89" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375004 1145 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z$, treating $x$ and $y$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,dz]dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-x-y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x(4-x-y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8x-2{x}^{2}-2xy)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8x-2{x}^{2}-2xy)\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8x-2{x}^{2}-2xy)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[8xy-2{x}^{2}y-x{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8x-2{x}^{2}-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(7x-2{x}^{2})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}7x-2{x}^{2}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{7{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375005 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375006 13194139535929 true] [17592186375005 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535929 true] [17592186375005 1041 "field-1" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375005 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375006 13194139535929 true] [17592186375005 1110 #uuid "b5ffd7db-1548-5621-80f2-3165aabe6ed3" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375006 1034 "\\frac{17}{6}" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375006 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535929 true] [17592186375006 1111 #uuid "ea868574-78ae-585e-971a-a3dae1771c43" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375007 1017 #edb/ref 17592186375008 13194139535929 true] [17592186375007 1017 #edb/ref 17592186375010 13194139535929 true] [17592186375007 1017 #edb/ref 17592186375012 13194139535929 true] [17592186375007 1089 "q-168621" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375007 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535929 true] [17592186375007 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the volume of the region shown above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Volume $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nLet $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate is ${∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{17}{6}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\text{curl}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩$" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375007 1113 #uuid "12d221b0-8d48-5bff-9a95-221cf2846fc4" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375007 1145 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ {z}^{2} & 2xy & x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xy)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(2xy-0)\\,i-({y}^{2}-2z)\\,j+(2y-0)\\,k \\\\  & =2xy\\,i-({y}^{2}-2z)\\,j+2y\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2xy,\\,2z-{y}^{2},\\,2y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375008 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375009 13194139535929 true] [17592186375008 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535929 true] [17592186375008 1041 "field-1" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375008 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375009 13194139535929 true] [17592186375008 1110 #uuid "caabcbe7-e42a-536e-969c-a619ef52c6ce" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375009 1034 "2xy" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535929 true] [17592186375009 1111 #uuid "a622775d-a79f-57fc-86c6-e42336367b6e" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375010 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375011 13194139535929 true] [17592186375010 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535929 true] [17592186375010 1041 "field-2" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375010 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375011 13194139535929 true] [17592186375010 1110 #uuid "25e41b5d-78dd-5d5c-a61a-90b2ff053c93" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375011 1034 "2z-y^2" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375011 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535929 true] [17592186375011 1111 #uuid "9627f583-6aa3-5ce2-b369-e20efe21cf90" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375012 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375013 13194139535929 true] [17592186375012 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535929 true] [17592186375012 1041 "field-3" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375012 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375013 13194139535929 true] [17592186375012 1110 #uuid "05cb673c-c2ec-53d3-97a6-0dffdc671a25" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375013 1034 "2y" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375013 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535929 true] [17592186375013 1111 #uuid "3c44f28b-b161-58d2-b2dd-b96c7d73145a" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375014 1017 #edb/ref 17592186375015 13194139535929 true] [17592186375014 1089 "q-168625" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375014 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535929 true] [17592186375014 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the volume of the region shown above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Volume $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nLet $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate is ${∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{17}{6}$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\n$\\text{curl}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2xy$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2z-y^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nLet $C$ be the boundary of the top (tilted) surface of our solid. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, as shown in the diagram. Use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation of the air along $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required circulation is ${∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot dr\\,={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375014 1113 #uuid "2835fb6e-1491-5ec3-8845-9b31e74ebdbf" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375014 1145 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C.$ One such surface is our top (tilted) surface ${S}_{T}$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4-x-y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-x-y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨2xy,\\,2z-{y}^{2},\\,2y⟩{|}_{z=4-x-y} \\\\  & =⟨2xy,\\,2(4-x-y)-{y}^{2},\\,2y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{2xy}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{8-2x-2y-{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{2y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{T}}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2xy)\\cdot (-1)-(8-2x-2y-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-1)+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/d0f9cb64d0ae1c3615df5a05c9f580bb9c74f6529efd732cfd83e1f4328ff8a3.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375016 13194139535929 true] [17592186375015 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535929 true] [17592186375015 1041 "field-1" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375015 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375016 13194139535929 true] [17592186375015 1110 #uuid "977e44ef-f1ba-589c-8034-63e32f6d1656" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375016 1034 "8-2x+2xy-y^2" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375016 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535929 true] [17592186375016 1111 #uuid "e7bc637a-6068-5d79-953d-64455b3cbd82" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375017 1017 #edb/ref 17592186375018 13194139535929 true] [17592186375017 1089 "q-168626" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375017 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535929 true] [17592186375017 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072211/assets/f47c5a0a1cc62bc6aca913adea9f496e1a666017ec1cf5610b01535bf497af0d.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the three-dimensional region of air shown above. All the surfaces of the region, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes. The velocity field of the airflow is defined by \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nFind the volume of the region shown above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Volume $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $3$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nLet $S$ be the boundary of the region. The flux of the vector field $F$ across $S$ represents the rate at which the air passes through the surface. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to write down the integral that gives this rate.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required rate is ${∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{17}{6}$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\n$\\text{curl}\\,F\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $⟨$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2xy$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2z-y^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$⟩$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nLet $C$ be the boundary of the top (tilted) surface of our solid. The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, as shown in the diagram. Use Stokes' theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation of the air along $C.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The required circulation is ${∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot dr\\,={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $8-2x+2xy-y^2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375017 1113 #uuid "3c010048-751c-55ee-bc2d-866d3ad0598c" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375017 1145 "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2xy-2x-{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[x{y}^{2}-2xy-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+8y]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-2x-\\frac{1}{3}+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{23}{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{23}{3}-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{23x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{3}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375018 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375019 13194139535929 true] [17592186375018 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535929 true] [17592186375018 1041 "field-1" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375018 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375019 13194139535929 true] [17592186375018 1110 #uuid "aa962c53-d8c5-5cd7-9e4b-a23355e805a9" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375019 1034 "\\frac{43}{6}" 13194139535929 true] [17592186375019 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535929 true] [17592186375019 1111 #uuid "73a632af-4cb5-52d4-abef-f6e5a8faf034" 13194139535929 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-168615" 17592186374995 "q-168615/field-1" 17592186374996 "q-168615/field-1/e819017e19dccac106b8bdd7a345917915a57ae6ae3646d73f366105b31b91f7" 17592186374997 "q-168616" 17592186374998 "q-168616/field-1" 17592186374999 "q-168616/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" 17592186375000 "q-168617" 175